## 7. Re-enablement Procedure

> Re-enablement requires **explicit approval** from CISO + HR Director and must not be performed by the First Responder alone.

### Mechanism A — Re-enable
1. Navigate to Conditional Access policy **`HR-Copilot-Emergency-Block`**.
2. Set **Enable policy** → **`Report-only`** (not Off — preserve the policy for future use).
3. Save and confirm.
4. Log re-enablement time and approvers in the incident ticket.

### Mechanism B — Re-enable
1. Coordinate with the development team to redeploy the Azure OpenAI deployment.
2. `TODO: Link to deployment Terraform/ARM template: <repo-url>`
3. Run post-deployment smoke tests before opening to users.
4. Log re-enablement time and approvers.

---

## 8. Quarterly Kill Switch Test

**Schedule:** Once per quarter (calendar invites to all First Responders)
**Environment:** Production Conditional Access in Report-only mode generates no user impact — test the toggle in a **non-production tenant** or with a **test security group** scoped version of the policy.

> **TODO:** Create a parallel policy `HR-Copilot-Emergency-Block-TEST` scoped to a test user group for safe quarterly drills.

### Test Checklist

- [ ] First Responder activates kill switch (Mechanism A) against test policy/group
- [ ] Confirm test user is blocked within **15 minutes** of drill start — record exact elapsed time
- [ ] Confirm logs are preserved (Sentinel / Splunk — no gaps)
- [ ] Confirm no in-progress session data was lost
- [ ] Re-enable policy and confirm test user access restored
- [ ] File test record in OneTrust GRC with:
  - Date, participants, elapsed time to block, pass/fail, observations
- [ ] Review and update this runbook if any steps were unclear or slow

**Quarterly test pass criteria:** Block confirmed in ≤ 15 minutes, logs intact, no data loss.

