age for model improvement'' is disabled; signed Data Processing Agreement (DPA) on file; Drata evidence artifact linking DPA and configuration proof.', 'Review OpenAI organization settings via API (`GET /organization`) or admin dashboard to confirm training opt-out is active; confirm a signed DPA with GDPR-compliant data processing addendum is on file.', 'not_checked', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'runbook', 'markdown', 'openai-dpa-and-data-controls-review.md', '# Control 8ad5f347-aa0c-4e7d-a6ae-717f69fdd27d: OpenAI Data Processing Terms & Controls

**Pillar:** 3 | **Requirement:** Required

**Objective:** Verify and document that OpenAI does not train on submitted data, minimizes retention, and discloses all sub-processors. This runbook outlines the manual review, DPA execution, and evidence-collection steps required to meet this control.

---

## Prerequisites

- Access to OpenAI platform admin dashboard (`platform.openai.com`)
- Authority to execute a Data Processing Agreement (DPA) on behalf of the organization
- Access to Drata GRC platform for evidence upload and vendor management
- OpenAI Account Owner or Admin role

---

## Step 1: Verify OpenAI Platform Data Controls

### 1.1 Log in to OpenAI Platform

1. Navigate to https://platform.openai.com/account/org-settings/general
2. Authenticate using organization credentials (SSO via Okta if available)
3. Confirm you have **Organization Owner** or **Admin** access

### 1.2 Disable "Improve our models"

1. In the left sidebar, click **Organization** → **Settings**
2. Locate the **Data & Privacy** or **Data Controls** section
3. Find the toggle labeled **"Improve our models"** or **"Allow usage for model improvement"**
4. **Confirm the toggle is OFF** (disabled)
   - ❌ If toggle is ON: immediately disable it and notify data governance team
   - ✅ If toggle is OFF: take a screenshot and proceed to Step 2
5. Document the date and user who verified this setting in the corresponding Jira ticket

### 1.3 Verify Retention Settings

1. In the same **Data & Privacy** section, confirm the data retention policy:
   - Expected: "API requests and data are retained for 30 days for security and abuse prevention only, then deleted."
   - If different, escalate to Data Governance
2. Screenshot the retention policy display and attach to evidence

---

## Step 2: Execute Data Processing Agreement (DPA) with OpenAI

### 2.1 Initiate DPA Request

1. Contact OpenAI enterprise support at **demo@community.example** with subject: **"DPA Request – GDPR Article 28 Processor Terms"**
2. Provide:
   - Organization name and entity details
   - Use case: LangChain-based agent queries to OpenAI API
   - Reference to your Privacy Policy and GDPR processing purpose
   - Request DPA covering:
     - GDPR Article 28 (Processor obligations)
     - Data sub-processor list and approval mechanism
     - Notification of processor changes
     - Data security and confidentiality terms
     - Audit rights and compliance assistance

### 2.2 Review OpenAI''s Standard DPA

1. OpenAI will provide their standard DPA template
2. Review with Legal and ensure it covers:
   - ✅ "Data submitted via the API is not used to improve OpenAI models" (explicit carve-out)
   - ✅ "Retention period: 30 days for abuse detection only"
   - ✅ "Sub-processor notifications and approval rights"
   - ✅ "Data subject rights support (export, deletion)"
   - ✅ Standard contractual clauses (SCCs) for international transfers (if applicable)
3. If gaps exist, propose amendments in writing

### 2.3 Execute and Archive DPA

1. Obtain Legal and Compliance sign-off
2. Execute DPA via e-signature (DocuSign, Adobe Sign, or OpenAI''s process)
3. Retain fully executed copy in secure archive (e.g., Google Cloud Storage with encryption)
4. Save a copy to the shared evidence repository for Drata upload (see Step 4)

---

## Step 3: Document OpenAI Sub-Processor List

### 3.1 Retrieve Sub-Processor List

1. Request OpenAI''s current sub-processor list (typically available at https://openai.com/privacy/ or provided in DPA)
2. Expected sub-processors may include:
   - Azure (cloud infrastructure hosting)
   - Stripe (billing, if applicable)
   - Third-party security/compliance vendors
3. Document the source and date retrieved

### 3.2 Verify Sub-Processor Scope

1. Confirm each sub-processor handles **only** technical operations (infrastructure, hosting, DDoS protection) and does **NOT** receive:
   - Raw query content beyond what is necessary for service provision
   - Retrieved documents or proprietary context
   - Any data used for training or product improvement
2. If any sub-processor has broader access, escalate immediately to Data Governance and OpenAI support

### 3.3 Document in Drata Vendor Record

See Step 4, Section 4.3.

---

## Step 4: Upload Evidence to Drata

### 4.1 Prepare Evidence Package

Gather the following documents:

1. **Executed DPA** (PDF, fully signed)
2. **OpenAI Sub-Processor List** (PDF or screenshot with date and source)
3. **Platform Settings Screenshot** showing "Improve our models" = OFF
4. **OpenAI API Data Usage Policy** (URL + screenshot or PDF from https://openai.com/api/policies/api-data-usage-policies/)
5. **Retention Policy Confirmation** (screenshot or extract from https://openai.com/policies/api-data-policies/)

### 4.2 Access Drata

1. Log in to Drata at https://app.drata.com/
2. Navigate to **Vendors** (or **Third-Party Risk** depending on Drata version)
3. Search for or create vendor record: **OpenAI**

### 4.3 Create/Update Vendor Record

1. **Vendor Name:** OpenAI
2. **Category:** SaaS / AI Services / Cloud Processing
3. **Risk Level:** High (processes queries and documents, LLM provider)
4. **Data Classification:** Confidential (business queries, potentially sensitive context)
5. **Compliance:** GDPR, check box if applicable
6. **Sub-Processors:** Upload or link sub-processor list and confirm reviewed
7. **Attestations & Agreements:**
   - Upload executed DPA
   - Link to or upload API Data Usage Policy
   - Retain version and effective date
8. **Last Review Date:** Today''s date
9. **Next Review Date:** 12 months from today (annual review cadence)

### 4.4 Add Linked Evidence

1. In Drata, create a linked control evidence record:
   - **Control ID:** 8ad5f347-aa0c-4e7d-a6ae-717f69fdd27d
   - **Evidence Type:** Vendor Agreement / Configuration
   - **Artifacts:**
     - DPA (link to Drata file or external archive)
     - Sub-processor list (snapshot as of date)
     - Platform configuration screenshot
     - API data usage policy URL and confirmation
   - **Owner:** Data Governance or Compliance team lead
   - **Sign-off:** Obtained by [TODO: your-name] on [TODO: date]

---

## Step 5: API Configuration Verification (LangChain Integration)

### 5.1 Confirm API Usage Policy

1. Retrieve OpenAI''s current API Data Usage Policy from:
   - URL: https://openai.com/api/policies/api-data-usage-policies/
   - Screenshot and retain as evidence
2. Confirm it explicitly states:
   - **"By default, data submitted via the API is NOT used to improve OpenAI models."**
   - No training, model optimization, or analytics usage (unless explicitly opted in)
3. If using the API, you should have **no** toggle to enable model training — if a toggle exists, escalate to OpenAI support

### 5.2 Document LangChain Agent Configuration

1. In the LangChain agent code repository (GitHub), confirm:
   - OpenAI API key is stored securely (e.g., Okta vault or GCP Secret Manager)
   - No custom telemetry or logging sends queries to unauthorized endpoints
   - OpenAI API endpoint is the official endpoint (api.openai.com)
2. Add a configuration comment or documentation:
   ```
   # OpenAI API is configured per DPA signed [DATE].
   # Data processing: Queries are NOT used for model training (per API policy).
   # Retention: 30 days for abuse prevention only.
   # Sub-processors: [Link to reviewed list in Drata]
   ```
3. Commit this configuration to the repository with reference to the control ID

---

## Step 6: Ongoing Monitoring & Annual Review

### 6.1 Set Review Reminder

1. Create a recurring Jira task (or Drata task) scheduled for **12 months from today**
2. **Title:** "Review OpenAI DPA & Data Controls – Control 8ad5f347-aa0c-4e7d-a6ae-717f69fdd27d"
3. **Description:** Repeat Steps 1–3 to confirm:
   - Platform settings remain disabled
   - DPA is still in effect and current
   - Sub-processor list has not changed materially
   - API policy has not been modified adversarially

### 6.2 Change Management

1. If OpenAI updates their DPA, sub-processor list, or data controls:
   - Notify Data Governance and Legal immediately
   - Conduct impact assessment
   - Update Drata vendor record within 5 business days
   - If new sub-processors: evaluate and approve per vendor risk policy

### 6.3 Incident Response

1. If "Improve our models" is ever found to be ON:
   - Disable immediately
   - Notify CISO and Data Governance
   - Log incident in Jira and CrowdStrike (if EDR integration available)
   - Assess exposure window
   - Report to affected stakeholders per Privacy Policy

