# Runbook: Azure OpenAI — GDPR Data Residency, No-Training, No-Retention & DPA Assurance

## Purpose
Confirm and produce auditable evidence that the Azure OpenAI Service resource processing HR personal data (including special-category data) is configured in accordance with GDPR obligations: EU data residency, no model-training on customer data, no prompt/completion retention, modified abuse monitoring, and a valid Microsoft Data Processing Agreement (DPA) in scope.

---

## Scope
- Azure OpenAI Service resource(s) used by the HR AI assistant
- Microsoft Customer Agreement (MCA) / Enterprise Agreement (EA) subscription
- OneTrust GRC vendor record for Azure OpenAI

---

## Prerequisites
| # | Requirement | Owner |
|---|-------------|-------|
| 1 | Azure Portal access (Reader + Azure OpenAI Contributor) | Platform Engineer |
| 2 | Access to Microsoft 365 Admin Center / Volume Licensing portal to locate DPA | Legal / Procurement |
| 3 | OneTrust GRC edit access for vendor records | Privacy / GRC team |
| 4 | Access to Azure OpenAI Limited Access / modified abuse monitoring request form | AI Platform Owner |

---

## Step 1 — Verify EU Data Residency of the Azure OpenAI Resource

```bash
# TODO: Replace <subscription-id> and <resource-group> with your actual values.
# TODO: Replace <aoai-resource-name> with your Azure OpenAI resource name.

az login
az account set --subscription "<subscription-id>"

az cognitiveservices account show \
  --name "<aoai-resource-name>" \
  --resource-group "<resource-group>" \
  --query "{name:name, location:location, sku:sku.name, kind:kind}" \
  --output table
```

**Expected output:** `location` MUST be one of:
- `westeurope` (West Europe — Netherlands)
- `francecentral` (France Central)
- `swedencentral` (Sweden Central)
- `norwayeast` (Norway East)
- `germanywestcentral` (Germany West Central)

> ⚠️ If `location` is outside the EU/EEA, the resource must be **redeployed** into an approved EU region before processing HR personal data. Raise a change request immediately.

**Evidence to capture:** Screenshot or JSON export of the resource overview blade showing `location`. Save as `evidence/aoai-resource-location-<date>.png`.

---

## Step 2 — Confirm No-Training and No-Retention Commitments

Microsoft''s standard Azure OpenAI Service terms (as of API version 2023-06-01-preview onward and the Azure OpenAI Data Privacy addendum) state:
- Customer data (prompts and completions) is **not used to train** Microsoft''s base models.
- Prompts and completions are **retained for up to 30 days** for abuse monitoring by default, then deleted.

### 2a — Validate service terms version in use

```bash
# Confirm the API version deployed models use supports current data privacy commitments.
az cognitiveservices account deployment list \
  --name "<aoai-resource-name>" \
  --resource-group "<resource-group>" \
  --output table
# TODO: Verify each model deployment version aligns with the current DPA-covered API version.
```

### 2b — Confirm "abuse monitoring" configuration (no human review of HR prompts)

By default, Azure OpenAI retains prompts/completions for up to 30 days and may allow Microsoft personnel to review content for abuse monitoring. For HR special-category data, this is incompatible with GDPR Article 9.

**Action required:**
1. Navigate to: https://aka.ms/oai/modifiedaccess (Azure OpenAI Limited Access — modified abuse monitoring form).
2. Apply for **"Modified abuse monitoring"** (disables human review of prompts/completions and reduces automated retention).
3. TODO: Record the submission reference number and approval date.
4. Once approved, Microsoft will configure the resource so that prompt/completion data is **not retained** beyond the immediate request/response cycle for abuse detection purposes.

> Microsoft''s modified abuse monitoring approval applies at the **Azure subscription + resource** level. Confirm the approval letter explicitly names the subscription ID and resource region.

**Evidence to capture:** Save the approval email/letter as `evidence/aoai-modified-abuse-monitoring-approval-<date>.pdf`.

---

## Step 3 — Verify the Microsoft Data Processing Agreement (DPA) Covers This Processing

### 3a — Locate the DPA
| Agreement type | Where to find DPA |
|---|---|
| Microsoft Customer Agreement (MCA) | Included by reference in MCA; access at https://www.microsoft.com/licensing/docs/customeragreement |
| Enterprise Agreement (EA) | Attachment to the EA / OST; accessible via the Volume Licensing Service Center (VLSC) |
| Microsoft Products and Services Agreement (MPSA) | Online Services Terms (OST) + DPA at https://www.microsoft.com/licensing/terms |

**Action required:**
1. Log in to the Microsoft admin portal or VLSC.
2. TODO: Download the current **Online Services Data Protection Addendum (DPA)** PDF and confirm it is the version dated on or after **May 2023** (which covers Azure OpenAI under "Azure Services").
3. Confirm the DPA lists your organisation as "Customer" and that Azure OpenAI / Azure Cognitive Services is included in the covered services schedule.
4. TODO: Record the Agreement Number / Enrollment Number for traceability.

**Evidence to capture:** Save as `evidence/microsoft-dpa-<agreement-number>-<date>.pdf`.

### 3b — Confirm GDPR Processor obligations are engaged
- Confirm Microsoft acts as **data processor** and your organisation as **data controller** for HR personal data sent to Azure OpenAI.
- Verify the DPA references **EU Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs)** (2021/914) or equivalent transfer mechanism if data may transit outside EEA.
- TODO: If using a third-party integration layer (e.g., middleware, orchestration service), confirm that layer is also covered under a DPA or SCCs.

---

## Step 4 — Network & Access Controls (Supporting GDPR Integrity/Confidentiality)

```bash
# Confirm the Azure OpenAI resource is NOT exposed to public internet without restriction.
az cognitiveservices account show \
  --name "<aoai-resource-name>" \
  --resource-group "<resource-group>" \
  --query "{publicNetworkAccess:properties.publicNetworkAccess, \
            networkAcls:properties.networkAcls}" \
  --output json
```

**Expected:** `publicNetworkAccess` = `"Disabled"` or network ACLs restrict access to known VNet/IP ranges only.

> TODO: If `publicNetworkAccess` is `"Enabled"` with no ACLs, raise a remediation ticket to restrict access via Private Endpoint or IP allowlist.

---

## Step 5 — Document in OneTrust GRC Vendor Record

1. Log in to **OneTrust GRC & Security Assurance Cloud**.
2. Navigate to: **Vendor Management → Vendors → Microsoft (Azure OpenAI)**.
   - TODO: Create the vendor record if it does not exist.
3. Update/confirm the following fields:

| OneTrust Field | Value to Record |
|---|---|
| Vendor Name | Microsoft Corporation — Azure OpenAI Service |
| Processing Activity | HR AI Assistant — prompt/completion processing |
| Data Categories | Personal data; Special category (health, ethnicity etc. as applicable) |
| Data Subjects | Employees, Contractors |
| Legal Basis | Article 6(1)(b)/(c) + Article 9(2)(b)/(h) as applicable |
| Transfer Mechanism | EU region deployment + EU SCCs via Microsoft DPA |
| DPA Reference | TODO: Insert agreement/enrollment number and DPA version date |
| Modified Abuse Monitoring Approved | Yes — TODO: Insert approval date and reference |
| Data Residency Region | TODO: Insert confirmed Azure region (e.g., West Europe) |
| Next Review Date | TODO: Set to 12 months from today |
| Evidence Links | evidence/aoai-resource-location-<date>.png, evidence/microsoft-dpa-<date>.pdf, evidence/aoai-modified-abuse-monitoring-approval-<date>.pdf |

4. Assign a **risk rating** and confirm all inherent/residual risks are reviewed by the Privacy Officer.
5. TODO: Obtain sign-off from DPO or Privacy Lead and record in the OneTrust task/workflow.

---

## Step 6 — Evidence Pack Summary

Assemble the following into a dated evidence package (e.g., stored in SharePoint under `Compliance/AI-HR/AzureOpenAI-GDPR-Evidence/`):

| # | Evidence Item | Format | Reviewer |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Azure OpenAI resource location screenshot/export | PNG / JSON | Platform Engineer |
| 2 | Azure OpenAI model deployment list | JSON | Platform Engineer |
| 3 | Modified abuse monitoring approval | PDF / Email | AI Platform Owner |
| 4 | Microsoft DPA (current version) | PDF | Legal / Privacy |
| 5 | Network ACL / Private Endpoint configuration export | JSON | Platform Engineer |
| 6 | OneTrust vendor record screenshot / export | PDF | GRC / Privacy Lead |
| 7 | DPO sign-off record | Email / OneTrust task | DPO |

---

## Periodic Review

| Cadence | Activity |
|---|---|
| **Annual** | Re-verify DPA version, re-confirm modified abuse monitoring approval, re-check region |
| **On change** | Any model version upgrade, new deployment, or subscription change triggers re-review |
| **On Microsoft terms update** | Monitor Microsoft Service Trust Portal for DPA amendments; update OneTrust record |

---

## Escalation
- If EU residency cannot be confirmed → **stop processing HR data** on that resource and escalate to DPO immediately.
- If modified abuse monitoring approval is denied → assess alternative mitigations (e.g., prompt anonymisation layer, alternative model provider) and document in OneTrust risk register.
- If DPA cannot be located → escalate to Legal/Procurement within **5 business days**.

---

